//   `site` + `base` in astro.config.mjs, and there is no CNAME anywhere in the repo, so
//   it is a GitHub Pages project site. Verified live 2026-09-14: HTTP 200 over TLS 1.3.
//   That is the hook: the padlock the students look at every day is their own course site,
//   not somebody else's example, and every field in Build Lab Step 2 is a field they can
//   open and read themselves.
// - Verified certificate on that host (checked 2026-09-14): subject CN=*.github.io — a
//   WILDCARD, not the site's name — issued by O=Let's Encrypt, valid 2 Aug -> 31 Oct 2026.
//   The dates and the issuer ROTATE (Let's Encrypt certificates are ~90 days), so the
//   lesson text says what was seen and tells the student to read their own rather than
//   copying a value that will be stale by the time they read it.
// - Bad-certificate demonstration: the old text sent students to a host under `.example`,
//   which is a reserved TLD (RFC 2606) that never resolves — that produces a DNS failure,
//   not a certificate refusal, so the promised "which step failed" lesson did not happen.
//   Replaced with self-signed.badssl.com, verified to present a genuinely self-signed
//   certificate ("SSL certificate problem: self signed certificate"), and expired.badssl.com.
// - RSA-2048 is a 617-digit modulus built from two primes of about 309 digits each. The old
//   text said "two 600-digit prime factors", which would be a ~1200-digit modulus — no
//   deployed key is that size.
// - TLS protects the channel, not the endpoints; a certificate asserts a name-to-key
//   binding and nothing about the site's honesty. Both are stated explicitly in the
//   callout, because they are the two the exam writes wrong options from.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';
import type { DecomposeSpec } from '../../lib/decompose';

const DECOMPOSE: DecomposeSpec = {
  big: 'A message has to cross a network anybody can listen to, and arrive readable by exactly one person. "Keep it secret" is a wish; the work is the five decisions underneath it.',
  frame: 'concept',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Choose the kind of lock: one shared key, or a pair',
      does: 'Symmetric is fast and needs both ends to already hold the same secret; asymmetric is slow and needs only that you published half of a pair. Real traffic uses both, for different jobs.',
      doc: 'the symmetric-versus-asymmetric table in this lesson\'s Concept Lab, and the two matching sections of the Encryption reference page',
      href: '../docs/unit-09/encryption.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Get the right public key into the other machine\'s hands',
      does: 'A public key on its own proves nothing — anybody can hand you a key and claim it belongs to the site. The certificate is the third party that says whose key this is.',
      doc: 'the certificate fields you read off your own site in Step 2 of the Build Lab below, and "What does a certificate prove?" on the Encryption reference page',
      href: '../docs/unit-09/encryption.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Trade one round of slow work for a whole session of fast work',
      does: 'The handshake: asymmetric to agree on a session key and to prove identity, symmetric for everything after it. One slow exchange buys the rest of the conversation at full speed.',
      doc: 'the six-step handshake in the Concept Lab above',
    },
    {
      name: 'Decide how many bits the key has to be',
      does: 'Key length is the whole cost of the attack. A 2048-bit RSA key is a 617-digit number built from two primes of about 309 digits: multiplying them is instant, pulling them back apart is not.',
      doc: 'the bit-width discussion in 9.1, and the RSA paragraph on the Encryption reference page',
      href: '9.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Say what the padlock protects, and what it leaves exposed',
      does: 'It protects the channel — the bytes between two machines — and not the endpoints. The server still reads everything you send it, and a hash is still not encryption.',
      doc: 'the packet journey in 9.8, and the Hashing section of the Encryption reference page',
      href: '9.8.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'The split is the whole lesson, because "keep it secret" is a wish and these five are decisions. ' +
    'Three of them are choices you make before a single byte moves — which lock, how many bits, what the ' +
    'certificate has to prove — and they are the three the exam asks about. The last one is the one ' +
    'everybody skips: knowing what the mechanism does NOT cover is what stops you trusting a green ' +
    'padlock further than it can carry you.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test: could you answer this part out loud, in one sentence, with no computer in front of you? ' +
    '"Use encryption" points at nothing. "Find out whose public key this is" points at a certificate you ' +
    'can open and read field by field.',
  connect: [
    'In 9.1 you counted in bits and worked out that a 32-bit `int` stops just past two billion. A 2048-bit key is a number from the same family, just far past anything you could write down — so why is its SIZE the whole security of the thing, and what would a 64-bit key mean for the attacker?',
    'In 9.3 the exam trap was calling encryption a kind of compression. Encryption is lossless — every bit comes back — but it does not make the file smaller either. Say in one line each what compression and encryption are actually promising you.',
    'In 9.8 your push crossed strangers\' machines as packets, and the honest conclusion was that any router on the path can read what it forwards. What does the handshake change about that — and what about the journey stays exactly the same?',
    'In 9.4 you opened a scene file and found plain text you could read and edit, because your whole project is data all the way down. Your private key is a file on a disk too. What does that tell you about where the security of the whole system actually lives?',
  ],
  turns: [
    'Your own course site is served over HTTPS, and the subject of its certificate is not the address you typed. What is the browser actually matching against the host name — and does that certificate tell you anything about whether the site is honest?',
    'TLS protects the channel, not the endpoints. Your game sends a high score to a leaderboard over HTTPS: name two things an attacker can still do even though the padlock is green.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>9.9 — Encryption: Keys, TLS &amp; Public Key</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    When you pushed to GitHub, the URL said https — and your repo
    crossed the public internet unread by every router along the
    way. The page you are reading right now is served the same way,
    and the padlock in your address bar is the thing this lesson
    explains. Today: how a secret crosses an open network twice, why
    two keys are better than one, and the one math fact protecting
    everything you own.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I keep a message secret in transit?', 3, 'concept')}

${renderDecompose('9.9', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Symmetric — one shared key.</strong> The Caesar
    cipher you probably did in elementary school: shift letters by
    a key (3: CAT → FDW). Same key encrypts and decrypts. Fast,
    simple, and cursed by one problem: to agree on the key, you must
    already have a secure channel. The internet never had one, and
    that is the whole reason symmetric encryption could not be the
    answer on its own.</p>

    <p><strong>Asymmetric — two keys, a public pair.</strong> In
    the 1970s, math (huge prime numbers) delivered the miracle: you
    publish a PUBLIC key anyone can use to lock messages to you,
    and keep a PRIVATE key that is the only thing that unlocks
    them. Publishing the lock does not reveal the key — and that is
    not a slogan, it is the shape of the pair. One half can only
    lock and the other half can only open, so publishing the locking
    half costs you nothing. That is why "public key encryption" is
    not a contradiction. RSA-2048: the public number is 617 digits
    long and was built by multiplying two primes of about 309 digits
    each. Multiplying them takes a microsecond; pulling them back
    apart would take the world's computers longer than your
    lifetime. The exam wants the IDEA, not the factoring.</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th></th><th>Symmetric (AES)</th><th>Asymmetric (RSA)</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Keys</td><td>One shared secret</td><td>Public + private pair</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Speed</td><td>Very fast</td><td>Slow</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Perfect at</td><td>Bulk data — the bytes you actually send</td><td>Proving identity and handing over a key — the handshake</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>TLS — the handshake (https).</strong> The web uses
    BOTH: asymmetric to establish trust, symmetric for the actual
    conversation. When you see the lock icon:</p>
    <pre><code>1. Browser → github.com:  "hello"
2. Server → browser:    certificate + its PUBLIC key
3. Browser checks the certificate against trusted authorities
4. Browser generates a random SESSION key, encrypts it with the
   server's public key, sends it
5. Server decrypts it with its private key — the ONLY one who can
6. Both switch to fast symmetric encryption with the shared key</code></pre>
    <p>Step 3 is what "certificate" means — a third party vouches
    that this public key really belongs to github.com. Without it,
    anyone on your wifi could impersonate the site and hand you
    THEIR key (a man-in-the-middle attack).</p>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Four sentences the exam writes its wrong answers from.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>"Public key encryption" is not a contradiction.</strong> The
            pair is not one secret written down twice. One half can only lock; only
            the other half opens. So publishing the locking half gives nothing away —
            and the public key <em>cannot</em> decrypt what it encrypted. That single
            fact is a whole exam question.</li>
            <li><strong>A certificate asserts exactly one thing:</strong> that the
            public key inside it belongs to the name inside it, according to the
            authority that signed it. It does not assert that the site is honest,
            careful, or run by the company you think it is. A green padlock says your
            bytes are going to the machine named in the certificate. It says nothing
            else, and it is not a safety rating.</li>
            <li><strong>TLS protects the channel, not the endpoints.</strong> The
            routers in between cannot read your push — but the server at the far end
            reads every byte of it, and so does anything downstream of that server.
            Nor is the connection invisible: somebody watching still sees which
            machine you contacted, when, and how much you sent.</li>
            <li><strong>A hash is not encryption.</strong> No key, no way back — a
            fingerprint rather than a lockbox. That is why a stolen password database
            is a different size of disaster from a stolen key, and why the fix is a
            salt rather than a longer key.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Why does TLS not simply use asymmetric encryption for
            everything (hint: your 2 MB project push at RSA speed)?
            The exam phrasing: "explain how symmetric and
            asymmetric encryption are EACH used in secure
            transactions" — write the one-sentence version now.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — caesar by hand.</strong> Encrypt "BOSS"
    with key 3 on paper (ERVV). Pass to a partner who decrypts.
    Then: which letter appeared twice in the ciphertext — and why
    does that PATTERN (frequency analysis) break Caesar without
    knowing the key at all? Weak ciphers die to the STATISTICS of
    language, not to brute force.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — read a real certificate.</strong> You do not
    have to go far for one: the page you are reading now is served
    over HTTPS, from <code>aramansell.github.io</code>. Click the
    padlock in the address bar and open the certificate. Then read
    its fields and say what each one is for: the <em>subject</em>,
    the <em>issuer</em>, the <em>valid dates</em>, and the public
    key info. Two of them will surprise you. The subject is not the
    address you typed — it reads <code>*.github.io</code>, a single
    wildcard certificate covering every GitHub Pages site — and the
    browser accepts it because the certificate's list of names
    matches the host you asked for. And the lifetime is short: the
    day this page was last checked the certificate ran from
    2 August to 31 October and was issued by Let's Encrypt, which
    are values you should expect to differ from yours. Read your
    own; do not copy mine.</p>

    <p>Then go and look at two that are deliberately broken.
    <a href="https://self-signed.badssl.com/">self-signed.badssl.com</a>
    presents a certificate signed by nobody at all, and
    <a href="https://expired.badssl.com/">expired.badssl.com</a>
    presents one whose dates have run out. Read the browser's
    refusal on each and find the field it names: that field is
    step 3 of the handshake above, failing in front of you. Do not
    click through either one — a certificate warning is the browser
    telling you step 3 did not pass.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — your git connection.</strong>
    <code>git remote -v</code> in your project. Is it an https or an
    ssh URL? Both are encrypted. SSH is the asymmetric idea with a
    different protocol: your ssh keypair IS your public/private
    pair (<code>~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub</code> vs the private file).
    If you set up GitHub Desktop, it did https + TLS. Either way,
    your pushes have been unread to every router
    (<a href="9.8.html">9.8</a>) since day one.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Crypto failures</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>You encrypt your save file with the key stored IN
                the game the players download. What did you
                actually build (obfuscation, not security) — and
                why does every "DRM cracked" headline follow from
                this exact design?</li>
            <li>The exam classic: a site gives every visitor the
                same secret key and encrypts all of their traffic
                with it. What breaks first — and why is "key
                distribution" the sentence that answers it?</li>
            <li>Your password vault uses one hash for every user's
                same password "password123." Why does a SALT (a
                random per-user value mixed in) change the
                stolen-database story completely?</li>
            <li>Quantum computing question the exam is probing at:
                if factoring becomes fast, what specifically dies
                (asymmetric) and what survives (symmetric) — and
                why is the industry switching standards NOW
                rather than later?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Threat modeling</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Threat-model your game: a hypothetical cloud
                leaderboard. What is the asset (scores), who are
                the attackers (cheaters), and which trust
                boundary must the server own (<a href="9.8.html">9.8</a>
                tinker answer, upgraded)?</li>
            <li>Write the exam answer: "Which is true about public
                key encryption?" — make up the two wrong
                options too (the exam is written by people who
                write wrong options; knowing WHY they are wrong
                is the skill).</li>
            <li>Hard: why can you not reverse a HASH (one-way
                function), and why does your password log in as
                a HASH comparison rather than a decryption?
                (Nothing was ever encrypted — it was
                fingerprinted.)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain symmetric vs asymmetric with a use case for each</li>
            <li>Narrate the TLS handshake's six steps and where certificates fit</li>
            <li>Read a real certificate and name what its fields prove</li>
            <li>Explain why hashing is not encryption, and what a salt buys</li>
            <li>Name the parts of keeping a message secret, in the order they are decided, and say where each one is written down</li>
            <li>Say what a certificate proves and what it does not, and why the public key cannot undo its own lock</li>
            <li>Explain why TLS protects the channel and not the endpoints</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

